<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/expert/synthetic-biology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Synthetic biology is <strong>engineering applied to living systems</strong>: designing new
    biological parts, devices and organisms to do useful, specified things. It treats DNA as
    programmable and cells as chassis — promising fuels, medicines and materials grown rather
    than manufactured. It also carries biology's sharpest ethical edge, because programmable
    life is dual-use by definition.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the engineering framework inside the biology" widget="equationStep" config={synbioCfg} />

  <h2>Landmarks and frontiers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimal genomes:</strong> in 2010 the J. Craig Venter Institute booted up a cell controlled by a chemically synthesised genome; in 2016 they built a bacterium with the smallest genome known to run (473 genes). Defining "the minimum required for life" is foundational science.</li>
    <li><strong>Biosensors:</strong> engineered bacteria that detect arsenic in water, glucose in blood, or inflammation in the gut — living diagnostics at near-zero cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Agriculture & climate:</strong> nitrogen-fixing cereal crops (to cut fertiliser), engineered algae for fuels, and enzymes designed to digest plastic waste — each a large-scale environmental bet.</li>
    <li><strong>The governance frontier:</strong> the 2018 CRISPR-babies scandal triggered international moratoria on heritable editing; biosecurity protocols now screen DNA orders for dangerous sequences. The field's history is a case study in capability racing ahead of consensus — and building the brakes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gibson et al., Science (2010); Hutchinson et al., Science (2016); National Academies, Biodefense in the Age of Synthetic Biology (2018).</p>
</TopicPage>
